  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 6,
    "lesson_title": "Interpreting Poles in Simple Transform Pairs",
    "lesson_core_move": "Map each pole in a simple one-sided Laplace transform pair to the corresponding time-domain component, interpreting its s-plane location as a constant term, decay rate, or growth rate.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Interpreting Poles in Simple Transform Pairs",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Interpreting Poles in Simple Transform Pairs",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In this topic, all inverse Laplace rules are one-sided. After inverting each transform term, include the causal window $u(t)$ so the result is a causal time-domain component.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use this matching move first: identify which denominator template a term uses, then map that denominator directly to its time-domain behavior. The three canonical templates are $\\frac{1}{s}$, $\\frac{1}{s+a}$, and $\\frac{1}{s-a}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{u(t)\\}=\\frac{1}{s},\\;\\operatorname{Re}(s)>0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A pole at $s=0$ corresponds to the constant template, a pole at $s=-a$ with $a>0$ corresponds to a decaying exponential, and a pole at $s=a$ with $a>0$ corresponds to a growing exponential. Keep this interpretation attached to the denominator sign.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{-at}u(t)\\}=\\frac{1}{s+a},\\;a>0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use scalar multiplication by inspection after classifying the pole: $\\frac{K}{s+a}$ inverts to $K e^{-at}u(t)$ (decay), and $\\frac{K}{s-a}$ inverts to $K e^{at}u(t)$ (growth). This is the same move for all subsequent steps, with only sign and coefficient changes.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{at}u(t)\\}=\\frac{1}{s-a},\\;\\operatorname{Re}(s)>a",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Next examples will reuse the same mapping and only vary two things: the pole location and the numerator scale $K$. Your job is to classify the term first, then scale.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Common mistake to avoid: flipping the pole sign or dropping $u(t)$. Any positive $a$ in $s+a$ is decay, and every one-sided inverse in this topic keeps the unit-step window.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Interpreting Poles in Simple Transform Pairs",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Mapping the Pole at $s=0$ to a Constant Time-Domain Component",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find $x(t)$ for $X(s)=\\frac{5}{s}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a single term with denominator $s$, so this is the constant-time template from the origin pole. Match $\\frac{1}{s}$ to $u(t)$, then keep the one-sided causal window on the result.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(s)=\\frac{5}{s}=5\\cdot\\frac{1}{s}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply linearity to move the scalar outside the inverse transform: $\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\left\\{X(s)\\right\\}=\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\left\\{5\\cdot\\frac{1}{s}\\right\\}=5\\,\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\left\\{\\frac{1}{s}\\right\\}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s}\\}=u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute the base inverse and report the final result as $x(t)=5u(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Interpreting Poles in Simple Transform Pairs",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Mapping a Negative Real Pole to a Decaying Exponential",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find $x(t)$ for $X(s)=\\frac{4}{s+3}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the next controlled step: the denominator is no longer just $s$, so classify the pole using the sign in $\\frac{K}{s+a}$ and keep the causal inverse form.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(s)=\\frac{4}{s+3}=\\frac{4}{s-(-3)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Read $s+3$ as $s-(-3)$, so the pole is at $s=-3$ in the left half-plane. That identifies the decaying template for the inverse.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\frac{1}{s+a}\\;\\longleftrightarrow\\;e^{-at}u(t),\\quad a=3",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply linearity to the leading coefficient: $x(t)=4\\,\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\left\\{\\frac{1}{s+3}\\right\\}=4e^{-3t}u(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Final result: $x(t)=4e^{-3t}u(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Interpreting Poles in Simple Transform Pairs",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Mapping a Positive Real Pole to a Growing Exponential",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $X(s)=\\frac{2}{s-5}$, find the inverse one-sided time-domain expression $x(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The new wrinkle is the pole sign: this term uses $\\frac{1}{s-a}$, so identify the pole and then map to a growth template.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(s)=\\frac{2}{s-5}=2\\cdot\\frac{1}{s-5}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Read the denominator as $s-a$ with $a=5$, so the pole is at $s=5$. This is the positive-real case, corresponding to growth.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\frac{1}{s-a}\\;\\longleftrightarrow\\;e^{at}u(t),\\quad a>0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the coefficient by linearity: $x(t)=2\\,\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\left\\{\\frac{1}{s-5}\\right\\}=2e^{5t}u(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Final result: $x(t)=2e^{5t}u(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Interpreting Poles in Simple Transform Pairs",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Comparing Pole Locations Across Simple Transform Pairs",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find $x(t)$ when $X(s)=\\frac{6}{s+3}+\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{2}{s-4}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This example extends to three terms at once, so classify each denominator separately and reuse the same mapping rule for each pole before recombining.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(s)=\\frac{6}{s+3}+\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{2}{s-4}=\\frac{6}{s-(-3)}+\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{2}{s-4}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Classify each term: $s+3$ gives a pole at $s=-3$ (decay), $s$ gives a pole at $s=0$ (constant), and $s-4$ gives a pole at $s=4$ (growth).",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\frac{6}{s+3}\\to 6e^{-3t}u(t),\\quad \\frac{1}{s}\\to u(t),\\quad -\\frac{2}{s-4}\\to -2e^{4t}u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now recombine by linearity and keep one-sided support on every component: $x(t)=6e^{-3t}u(t)+u(t)-2e^{4t}u(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The final time-domain expression is $x(t)=6e^{-3t}u(t)+u(t)-2e^{4t}u(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
